A||N||15||Jason Kamrud||Poor college boy wants to install V-8 for cheap...||||6||
Z||000000||Jason Kamrud||05-11-2001||06:17 PM||||I just got done with college for the year & I only plan on working 40 hrs/week this summer, so I need a summer project.  I have a '86 GT fiero that I want to swap a small-block in for REALLY cheap.  The 2.8 / 4-spd Muncie is fun to rip around in, but I really miss the feeling of 'real' horsepower.<P>  I have a mild 283 that I freshly rebuilt, and also I have the bottom end of a 302 DZ ('67 small-journal) with forged TRW pistons. I really like the short-throw forged cranks b/c they're tougher than nails.  Now the 283 I built should be right around 250-257 HP, but if I built the 302 I would have 350+ hp AND a 7000+ rpm redline, which would be a real screamer in a 4-speed. Also with a car weighing &lt;3000 lbs, I don't think I would need a whole lotta low-end grunt to have some fun.  This car would be a daily driver, but I might possibly take it on our local 1/8 mile if I get the chance.  Any suggestions?  I would appreciate any information.<P>I would like to know if any of you guys have built adapter plates for a SBC.  I was trying to decide if like a 1/4 or 3/8 plate steel would do it...I'd hate to have to space it any more than that. Now it's been a long time since I've pulled an engine out of a Fiero, and I'm also wondering if the thickness of the plate will make a deeper pilot bushing necessary...? I was thinking that a roller pilot-bearing would be a good idea, that way I would have something solid to seat the input shaft into just in case my bellhousing bolt holes aren't 100% accurate.  Also I would like to know if you guys actually use any type of a GM production clutch setup, or if you go for the 'made in Japan' aftermarket or custom types.  Remember, I am on a very tight, college-friendly budget. VERY tight, tight-- like angle-iron bolted thru hockey pucks for motor mounts(hey, I've done it before!)--that kind of tight.  I've seen what Archie & Zumalt want for their kits & it's outrageous.  I can't justify spending a couple grand on a car I paid 500 bucks for right now, so I'll do as much as possible by myself.<P><BR>Any advice would be greatly appreciated!!<P>Thanks!<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jason Kamrud<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000001||Richard Parnell||05-11-2001||07:29 PM||||Good luck.  It won't be cheap.  The adapter plate from Archie I have is at least 3/4 of an inch thick.||||reg||1
Z||000002||V8Fiero||05-11-2001||08:55 PM||||Welcome to the forum. I used to be the asst manager at Pamida from 91 to 94 in Bottineau.<P>There is no such thing as a cheap V8 install. Ive been working on a so called budget install and the nickel and dime crap will kill ya........<P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000003||Dave Gunsul||05-11-2001||09:56 PM||||A 302 Chevy, my favorite little engine!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Do NOT let that engine go to waste!!<BR>Archies kit is top-notch so suck it up and<BR>buy the master kit and throw some Trick Flow 23 degree alum. heads on the 302 and the Sanderson headers and go to town! That thing will scoot! Just promise me that I get a ride in it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>BTW Comp Cams makes an improved cam for the 302.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000004||bubbajoexx||05-11-2001||10:00 PM|||| <A HREF="http://members.fortunecity.com/bublucy/" TARGET=_blank>http://members.fortunecity.com/bublucy/</A> <P>cheep check it out<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000005||Wannabe||05-11-2001||10:55 PM||||Ohhfff! 302 small block!!! That is my ultimate goal for a swap. 302 short block with aluminum heads and fuel injection. Those engines love to rev and are super strong. 7000 rpm redline is a piece of cake. You'd be able to show up all the Honda dudes by revving higher and having almost three times the displacement.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Archie||05-11-2001||11:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by bubbajoexx:<BR><B> <BR>cheep check it out</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I think he wants it Cheap not cheep.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Philphine||05-12-2001||08:09 AM||||i don't know enough about engines. i have read about how high the old 302's will rev. that sounds like it would be nice with a 4:10 trany, but hard to find and expensive if it were found. what's the difference bettween a 302 and a newer 305? could you get a 305 to redline the way you're saying the 302 will?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mathias||05-12-2001||10:27 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Wannabe:<BR><B>7000 rpm redline is a piece of cake. You'd be able to show up all the Honda dudes by revving </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Errm... Think again, 7k is [i]average[/b] for a Honda engine. Screaming to 8k is not unusual. Myself, I just finished work on my Miata where I'm at 7200rpm max before the limiter kicks in.<P>Back on topic, I'm definitely interested in building up my '86 GT and putting a 302 in it as well. If you come across any cheap idea's, lemme know.. I've been contemplating the cheap archie kit (basic kit.)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Vader||05-12-2001||12:13 PM||||Jason,<P>The 302 is probably the better choice.  For those of you not old enough to remember, the original '68 302 with solids and <I>NO</I> rev kit were very comfortable at 7,600 RPM, right from the factory.  The 4.88 gearing was almost ideal in the ¼ mile with that engine in an 'F' car.  The 'P' car should be that much better, and the lower gearing should be offset by the weight reduction.<P>The 302 is essentially the same bore/stroke as the base 151 Poncho engine for the Fiero, but has more holes.  Forget the Trick Flows for now, they'll blow your budget and won't gain you enough HP/$$ to make it worth the effort.  Ported stock heads can do much better if the cores aren't heavily shifted.<P>And as for the ricer engines revving to 8K RPM, remember that they are making less than 150 Lb/Ft of torque at that RPM, so the power output is actually fairly low compared to a <B>real</B> engine.  The stock 302 SBC at 7,000 RPM would still put 210 Lb/Ft out the crank - and that's STOCK.  You figure the HP from there...<P>------------------<BR>"<I>Make Me Bad</I>"||||reg||1
Z||000010||ray b||05-12-2001||04:21 PM||||302= 327 block with 283 crank wide bores short stroke z28 motor, race tuned made way more than rated 295 hp solid lifters are a pain thats worse in a fiero[no room to adjust]<BR>no the 305 ain't the same nowhere near the 302.<BR> i had a 307 with 302 heads but no hp or hi rev's[got it that way in a cheap el-c]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Wannabe||05-12-2001||06:32 PM||||Camshaft technology has come a long way since '69 and it is possile to use hydraulic lifters and run at 7000 rpm. To go above that regularly is probably another story, but a s far as the bottom end, 7000 really is a piece of cake. The 305 will not rev like a 302 because is uses a smaller bore and a longer stroke. It's not possible to use the large valve (2.02 intake) Chevy heads with a high lift cam on the 305. The power potential and flexibility of the small block continues to amaze me.||||reg||1
Z||000012||bubbajoexx||05-12-2001||08:37 PM||||the 302 chevy engine is easy to copy all you need is a small jounal327 block pre 68 and a 283 crank and rods then just buy replacment pistons for the 302 and have them put onto the 283 rods the 302 is a 4inch bore and a 3inch stroke you can also use the 350 4 bolt block if you use paw bearing spacers to fit the small jounal crank.<P>[This message has been edited by bubbajoexx (edited 05-12-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by bubbajoexx (edited 05-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Dave Gunsul||05-13-2001||06:23 PM||||The Trick Flow heads probably will blow your budget but they will make a very noticable difference in power and they will weigh 45-60<BR>pnds less which will help in not only straight line but will help you keep your good handling.<P>BTW don't forget to use headers!<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000014||Butter||05-14-2001||11:50 AM||||Jason,<BR>Had the same idea as you a few years back.  After I reseached the idea, I found Archie's site and decided to go with the Econo kit.  I didn't keep up with all the exspenses but a basic breakdown from memory is something like: $850 for the basic kit, $300 for CSI waterpump, $300 for radiator, $100 dual exhaust, $200+ for misc. stuff (starter and remote oil filter mount type stuff), and I had the body and engine already.  I basically saved a $1000, started by buying the kit and working on the car while putting back some $$ for the needed items to be.  Archie's not only sells quality products but helped with any questions I had and was nice about it.  As far as building the parts needed to fit in a SBC, if you don't have a good machinst friend, I say you'd be hard pressed to beat Archie's basic kit.  I have good machinst friends and I elected to go with Archie's kit do to errors that I may encounter that he has already solved.  Point in fact is that pilot bearing stuff you talked about, the Fiero has no pilot bushing. Things like that you wont have to hassle with Archie's kit.  I cheated on other things to bring my cost down, like starter, remote mounted oil filter, and exhaust cost.  Wish you well in your endeavors.   ||||reg||6
Z||000015||Jason Kamrud||05-15-2001||08:58 PM||||Thanks for all your quick responses!<P> Most of you guys recommended going with a kit already on the market like what Archie has, but I really can't afford to dish out $1000 for my secondary car...I'm currently restoring my '72 Cutlass, and all those OEM parts I've been buying really hurt the pocketbook!  So I still plan on trying to build an adapter setup myself, basically on trial & error, and we'll see what happens.<P>  I still believe that I can get by with a 3/8" thick steel adapter plate, and with that I wouldn't need a starter adapter...the starter drive will just have to engage a little farther.  I will have to see what can be done with the clutch setup...I might end up using the stock clutch & drill out a SBC flywheel for it, balance it, and call it good.  I do have ALOT of older GM parts cars, and alot of the parts interchange well, so I should be able to come up with something.  I don't think I'm going to go with a 302 just yet...I'll probably stick to the tough ol' 283 that's already built & ready to go. I'd hate to put my 302 on the line just in case I have some difficulties.<P>Again I would like to thank all of you for your input, and I will keep you posted with all of my progress!<P><BR>p.s.  One question to get me started before I tear things apart: Does anyone out there know off-hand the diameter & splines on the 4-speed muncie input shaft (or on the pressure disk if anyone has one lying around)?<P>------------------<BR>Jason Kamrud||||reg||14
